1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
And now it's the old Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Hey, did I ever tell you the one about the Scoutmaster's bunk at summer camp?

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Well, it hasn't been made up yet.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Oh yeah, it's really, it's come to this.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Hey, this is podcast number 365..

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Hey,

7
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Well, welcome back to the Scoutmaster podcast.

8
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,000
This is Clark Green.

9
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,000
We've gone through three or four podcasts in our foundation series

10
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,000
And over the next three we're going to wind it up by looking at

11
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,000
and talking about three main things: about scouting,

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,000
about playing the game, patrols, advancement and youth leadership,

13
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,000
And these are the key pieces that occupy most of what we do as

14
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Scouters and make scouting what it is for young people.

15
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,000
What we're going to do is ask ourselves why they're part of scouting,

16
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,000
how they are intended to function and what our role is in making them happen.

17
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
This week we're going to be talking about patrols.

18
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Remember to get in touch with me with your comments and questions.

19
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,000
I'll tell you how to do that towards the end of the podcast,

20
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,000
But for now, let's get started, shall we?

21
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,000
And now, ladies and gentlemen, a multifaceted man of many spigots,

22
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
a human soup bean, and they give him on his own time.

23
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
There's a quartermaster. everybody knows.

24
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
There's a song about him. this is how it goes.

25
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Just a little diddy doesn't mean a thing,

26
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,000
But when the boys are marching how they love to sing,

27
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:13,000
There were mice, mice eating up the rice in the stores.

28
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
In the stores there were rats- rats big as blooming cats.

29
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
In the quartermaster store.

30
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,000
My eyes are dim, I cannot see.

31
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
I have not brought my specs with me.

32
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:37,000
I have not brought my specs with me.

33
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:46,000
If you'll remember, one of the things that I've said somewhere within this series of podcasts

34
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:51,000
is that Scouting presents us with one goal and one method.

35
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:56,000
The goal is to develop character. the method is the patrol method,

36
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,000
And it's likely I'm going to do what I always do,

37
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
and that is use the terms patrol method and patrol system interchangeably.

38
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,000
But in a little bit I'm going to differentiate between those two terms.

39
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,000
But just stick with me while we figure a couple of things out first.

40
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,000
So step one is asking the question: why do we even have patrols?

41
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
And when I first became a Scouter, I looked at the whole patrol method thing

42
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,000
and was happy to have it operate in this kind of vestigial manner

43
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
that the Scouter's prior to me had been doing,

44
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:34,000
which is it's basically a way to administer a larger group

45
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
by dividing it into smaller groups.

46
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,000
that seemed to be the reason that we had it.

47
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
But if you look back at our history,

48
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:46,000
and you just look back at the principles upon which Scouting was founded,

49
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,000
you see that this is not the key reason.

50
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:52,000
even though it makes sense administratively to take a big group of people

51
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,000
and divide them into smaller groups to better manage them,

52
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
that wasn't why we started having patrols.

53
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:00,000
So let's jump into the way back machine here for a moment

54
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:03,000
and look at what happened when Scouting started.

55
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,000
And forgive me if you've heard all this before,

56
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:09,000
but Baden Powell, the founder of Scouting, is a British Army officer in Africa

57
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,000
during the Boer War.

58
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:15,000
He achieves a certain fame back home as a war hero.

59
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,000
He writes a book about the methods that he used in South Africa

60
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,000
and he calls those methods Scouting.

61
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,000
He comes home to Great Britain to find that people involved with youth

62
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:32,000
are using this book and is encouraged to write. Scouting for Boys

63
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
and foments a worldwide phenomenon.

64
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,000
There is no Scouting movement.

65
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,000
There is no Scouting organization.

66
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,000
There's only the book and the idea.

67
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Basically, what happens is young people get the book

68
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
and they just start doing Scouting.

69
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
and the way that they do that is by making up a patrol,

70
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:53,000
and there's a great story about this,

71
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,000
told by a fellow named John Thurman,

72
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,000
who was one of those young people,

73
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,000
and I want to read you some of that story.

74
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:05,000
Thurman wrote: in the early days of Scouting,

75
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,000
boys from all over the country, and later from all over the world,

76
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
bought the book Scouting for Boys

77
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,000
and formed themselves in patrols.

78
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:15,000
I want to repeat, formed themselves.

79
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
This means that someone- a leader,

80
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,000
not appointed by anyone in particular,

81
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,000
but chosen by other fellows

82
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,000
because those same other fellows were willing to follow him-

83
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
gathered around himself a crowd of chaps

84
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
who wanted to be scouts.

85
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,000
They formed a patrol and they started to train themselves

86
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,000
using the book Scouting for Boys as the only God.

87
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
but they found- as patrols have always found-

88
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,000
that there were many things they did not know,

89
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,000
many things they could not find out

90
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,000
and many things they could not do

91
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
without the help of some adult.

92
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:50,000
so the practice grew of a number of patrols

93
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:52,000
getting together, forming a troop

94
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,000
and usually finding their own scout master.

95
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,000
So this is why we have patrols,

96
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,000
because patrols are the basic unit of Scouting.

97
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
We didn't begin with troops and with adults being involved.

98
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
we began with young people taking the book,

99
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
forming themselves into patrols

100
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
and then finding out that

101
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
well, we could have some advantages

102
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
if adults were involved with this

103
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
and we would have the benefit of scale

104
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
if we brought our patrols together into a troop.

105
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
Now the rest of this story is told

106
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,000
in a post on ScoutMasterCGcom

107
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
called Why Do We Have Scout Masters?

108
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,000
and I will link to it in the podcast notes.

109
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
but in our discussion of the patrol method

110
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,000
or system,

111
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:35,000
I think this is all very helpful

112
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,000
in understanding how to

113
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,000
apply the patrol method.

114
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,000
See, Baden Powell

115
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:43,000
saw that young people

116
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
formed their own little gangs and groups

117
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,000
and thought that this would work out well

118
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
and set the number at around eight

119
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,000
because that just seemed to work really well.

120
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,000
So the patrol was just the natural

121
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,000
affiliation group

122
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,000
that a young person would normally form anyway,

123
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,000
and this was the basis

124
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:04,000
of the entire Scouting movement.

125
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Now, if you've been around for a while,

126
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,000
you've probably heard these two terms-

127
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,000
patrol system and patrol method,

128
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:12,000
and most of the time when we talk about it

129
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,000
they're kind of interchangeable terms.

130
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
I want to differentiate between those two terms

131
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:22,000
and separate them into two different approaches,

132
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,000
and you're not going to find this

133
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,000
in the literature anywhere.

134
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,000
This is just my own way of looking at it.

135
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,000
What's the difference between a patrol system

136
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,000
and a patrol method.

137
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,000
I'm going to say that we can use those terms

138
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:38,000
as shorthand for two different approaches

139
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,000
to how we work with patrols.

140
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:43,000
If we work with the patrol system,

141
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:46,000
we're paying attention to the administrative idea

142
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,000
of having a big group

143
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:50,000
split into smaller groups,

144
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,000
represented by a central body,

145
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:54,000
and it's purely an administrative tool

146
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:57,000
used for convenience and efficiency.

147
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:00,000
In contrast, when we talk about patrol method,

148
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
we're talking about the processes

149
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,000
of discovery and development that happen

150
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,000
within a patrol

151
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,000
or a small group,

152
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,000
because the patrol method

153
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,000
doesn't just apply to young people

154
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
in a scout troop,

155
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,000
it also applies to

156
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:20,000
cubs and dens and venturers and crews.

157
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:22,000
The system part

158
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:24,000
has different administrative terms,

159
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,000
names and practices,

160
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:28,000
but the method part is the same.

161
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:31,000
So let's think about the method.

162
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,000
Back to Thurman's story.

163
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
In the story

164
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
Thurman talks about like their first camping trip

165
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:38,000
and it's hilarious.

166
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
They made plenty of mistakes,

167
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,000
they tried out a lot of things

168
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:43,000
with mixed success.

169
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,000
So what happens in patrols

170
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:50,000
is there's a thousand little

171
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
interactions and things

172
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,000
are popping and moving along

173
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
and this is the way

174
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
character is developed,

175
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,000
or you end up

176
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
with the Lord of the Flies kind of situation

177
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:07,000
where things go downhill very quickly.

178
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
So our role is to

179
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
make sure the first situation

180
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
happens and prevent the second.

181
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,000
and the way that we do this is how?

182
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,000
think about it for a moment.

183
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:18,000
It's an easy answer.

184
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:19,000
It's the ideals of the scout,

185
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:20,000
oath and law.

186
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:21,000
It's encouraging our scouts

187
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
to follow those ideals

188
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
and put them into practice

189
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:26,000
in all of the interactions

190
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:27,000
and things that they have

191
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:28,000
with their patrol.

192
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:29,000
Now, if you've been following

193
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:30,000
through this series,

194
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
you've gotten the sense-

195
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:33,000
along with me, hopefully-

196
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
that all of these things

197
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
are incredibly simple things

198
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
and a lot of times simple things

199
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
have this kind of oxymoronic

200
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
nature to them where they are

201
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
the most durable. yet

202
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
they're also the most fragile

203
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
things about a given system.

204
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,000
As scouting developed

205
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
into a worldwide movement,

206
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
the organizational imperatives

207
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
began to change and morph

208
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:01,000
and usurp, in some cases,

209
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
some of the original intent

210
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:06,000
in the way which this was all set up.

211
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,000
So a digression for a moment

212
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:09,000
to explain myself,

213
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
because I've talked about

214
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
scouting organizations in all of this,

215
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
in these foundational series,

216
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:16,000
a couple of times.

217
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:17,000
So let's be very clear

218
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
about exactly what I'm saying.

219
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
You've no doubt come into scouting

220
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,000
through some kind of

221
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
an organizational setup.

222
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
If you're in the United States of America,

223
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:29,000
you've probably come in

224
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:30,000
through the Boy Scouts of America

225
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
or the Girl Scouts of America.

226
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
we have to understand that those

227
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
organizational things

228
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
are going to warp and obscure

229
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
some of the more simple things,

230
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
because keeping organizations

231
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
aimed at the individual

232
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:46,000
in the small group is a really difficult thing to do.

233
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
Now, interestingly,

234
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
none of what I have shared with you

235
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
runs counter

236
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
to the policies and rules

237
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
and procedures of any organization

238
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:58,000
that I know of,

239
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
and, at the same time,

240
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:01,000
most of what I've said

241
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
does run counter

242
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:04,000
to the culture

243
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
that's been established within

244
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:07,000
in those organizations

245
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:08,000
about what scouting is

246
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:09,000
and how it functions.

247
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:10,000
I think it's important enough

248
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
for me to repeat that

249
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
None of the advice that I'm giving you,

250
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
none of the

251
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
way that I'm describing

252
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
how scouting works in this

253
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:21,000
foundational series,

254
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
is intended to,

255
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
nor, in fact, does

256
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
run counter to any of the policies,

257
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
procedures or rules

258
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,000
of any scouting organization that I know of,

259
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
but it often does

260
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,000
run counter to the culture

261
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:39,000
that that organization has created.

262
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
The culture that organizations create

263
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
is kind of a macrocosm

264
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
of the things

265
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
that each of us as individuals

266
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
brings to the scouting program.

267
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
If you have a background in academia,

268
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
if you have a background in business,

269
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
if you have a background in the military,

270
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
if you have a background in the trades,

271
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
you're going to bring certain

272
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
understandings of the way an organization

273
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:02,000
operates,

274
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,000
whether it's a national organization

275
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
or a local organization,

276
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
or your scouting unit,

277
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:09,000
whether it be a cub pack,

278
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:10,000
a scout troop

279
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
or a venturing crew.

280
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
you know, if I

281
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
put you into a room full of 40 young people

282
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:17,000
and I said, okay,

283
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
present them a scouting program,

284
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
while you would begin with the way

285
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:22,000
that you manage things,

286
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
with your understanding

287
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
of how to bring order

288
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:27,000
and effectiveness

289
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
to the way that this is all going to work.

290
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
so those are all administrative

291
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
and organizational imperatives.

292
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
but the patrol method

293
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
goes a whole lot deeper

294
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
and it's a whole lot more important

295
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
than those surface things

296
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:44,000
and its importance is its simplicity

297
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
because I will say,

298
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
I'll repeat myself again-

299
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
the real reason that we want to have patrols

300
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
is that the interactions

301
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
within that patrol,

302
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
when they are based on the ideals of the scout,

303
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:57,000
oath and law,

304
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
is the key way,

305
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
the key stage

306
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
for character to be developed in our scouts.

307
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
and we've been encouraged through training

308
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:08,000
to examine the way small groups work

309
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,000
through some stages of development

310
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
like forming and norming and storming,

311
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
and it's all rhyming and it's great.

312
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:15,000
but forget about it,

313
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
because

314
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
the intent and reason

315
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:20,000
that we have patrols

316
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
is not that they become a high

317
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:25,000
performing subset of a broader organization.

318
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
now, that's very likely to happen anyway

319
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
without our intervention.

320
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:32,000
but that's not the reason that we have them.

321
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
remember why do we have them?

322
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
everyone?

323
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
because that's the environment in which

324
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
character is built.

325
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
and when you examine what a patrol does

326
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
and how they do it,

327
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,000
you will likely be

328
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,000
dissatisfied

329
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
and you need to manage that

330
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
dissatisfaction

331
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
by living with it,

332
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:55,000
mostly

333
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
because this is not a flaw

334
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
of the patrol method.

335
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
this is a feature

336
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
of the patrol method.

337
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
a patrol full of young people

338
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
is pretty much always storming,

339
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
is pretty much always chaotic,

340
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
is pretty much always searching for

341
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
even the basic

342
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
elements of organization

343
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:18,000
and coherence and skill.

344
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,000
and it's fine,

345
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
that's the way it's supposed to be,

346
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
because it is within that search,

347
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:27,000
it is within that process of discovery

348
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:30,000
that our scouts develop a character.

349
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
so here's a t-shirt that you can get

350
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:35,000
and hand it out to all the adults in your troop.

351
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
it says: don't fix that,

352
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:40,000
leave it alone.

353
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
the game is being played,

354
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:43,000
it's working.

355
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
this is how things happen.

356
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:46,000
this is what we're aimed at.

357
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:47,000
this is what we want to see.

358
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
I'm not talking about

359
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
anarchy and violence and horror shows-

360
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
those you stop-

361
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
but just the day to day,

362
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
the feeling around

363
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
trying to figure out what's going on,

364
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
the process of discovery,

365
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
of working together,

366
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
of resolving conflicts,

367
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
of trying to figure out

368
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
what leadership means

369
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
and how to follow leaders,

370
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
and how to put one foot in front of the other

371
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
and one stick at a time

372
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
on the fire-

373
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
all of those things

374
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
that we think we ought to fix

375
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
are the heart and soul

376
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
of what we really want for our scouts.

377
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
that thought

378
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
perhaps embodies

379
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
